WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
In today's video, I'm breaking down the

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.040
eight things that I stopped buying as I

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.760
got a little bit older. So, I recently

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.720
turned 30 years old and looking back at

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.520
my 20s, there were a lot of things that

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.920
I wish I did differently. I spent a lot

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.480
of money on things that I didn't need

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.960
to. And as someone who's a business

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.000
owner and entrepreneur that makes really

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.880
good money, I thought I deserved these

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.800
things. But in reality, they just slowed

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.760
down my progress of building actual

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.280
wealth or at the very minimum just not

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.160
being stressed out all the time about

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.480
money and actually having a good amount

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.280
of savings and investments. So, let's go

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.600
ahead and get into it and at any point

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.200
throughout the video, if you think I've

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.800
earned it or you've gotten value, do me

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.560
a huge favor and just hit that like

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.320
button. It helps the channel out a ton

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.440
and I greatly appreciate it. Okay, so

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.040
the first thing is probably going to

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.920
bother some people. Now, I'm not a

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.000
sports fan in general and that's really

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.320
my personal preference. I genuinely

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.240
don't understand though how people go

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.360
out and they spend hundreds or thousands

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.640
of dollars to watch other people do

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.240
their job and live their dreams. Think

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.920
about that for a second. You are paying

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.000
a significant amount of money, taking

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.600
time out of your day to sit in the

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.640
stands while somebody else performs and

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.200
builds their dreams. The average NFL

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.240
ticket costs $150. And if you add

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.960
parking, food, drinks, and maybe a

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.960
jersey, you're walking out of that

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.160
stadium having spent three, four, five

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.640
hundred bucks for a single afternoon.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.480
And if you're going to multiple games in

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.800
a year or a season, that's a car

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.480
payment. That's an investment

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.320
contribution. That's money that could

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.360
literally be building and compounding

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.440
over time. Because what I've noticed is

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.520
the same people dropping $400 on a

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.600
Sunday afternoon at the stadium are the

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.880
same people telling me that they can't

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.680
afford to invest in save. And a bonus

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.640
thing that ties right in is sports

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.560
betting. Almost a third of sports

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.640
bettors say that they have debt

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.040
attributed to gambling. About 45% say

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.360
they haven't saved up enough emergency

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.640
funds to cover three to six months of

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.560
living expenses. The amount that many

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.080
financial experts recommend. Yet,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.680
they're spending their time and their

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.840
money essentially gambling on sports.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.600
And again, it's just not contributing to

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.120
your future. So, the second thing is

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.800
buying new cars. So, in my late teens

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.479
and my 20s, I'm not going to lie. I

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.200
purchased a lot of cars. In fact, I'm 30

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:10.000
years old right now, and I've owned over

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.040
25 cars in my life. And out of the cars

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.640
that I've owned, I've only ever made

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.240
money on two of those cars. Now, both of

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.120
them were Lamborghinis, and I just

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.200
happened to buy them at the right time

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.920
and sell them at the right time. Every

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.680
other car that I've owned, I've lost

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.480
money on it. And looking back, that was

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.360
really stupid of me. Here's a number

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.760
that should stop you in your tracks. The

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.640
average American will spend $650,000

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.400
on cars over their lifetime. And the

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.520
worst part, new cars lose around 20% of

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.000
their value in the first year alone. And

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.040
after 5 years, they're worth about 40%

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.680
of what you paid. You are literally

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.920
paying a premium to take a loss. Let

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.200
somebody else absorb that loss and buy

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.880
the car 2 to 3 years old. A lot of

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.959
times, they're still under warranty,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.720
they have low miles, and it was probably

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:57.560
a one-owner car. I now drive a 2013

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.800
Audi, and it runs perfectly fine. It

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.920
gets me from point A to point B. I don't

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.480
have a car payment, and now I choose to

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.200
invest that money into things like

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.600
Bitcoin, the stock market, my

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.000
businesses, and real estate. All right,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.239
the third thing that I stopped buying

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.840
was new clothes all the time. I realized

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:14.720
that the people who are always buying

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.800
new and expensive clothing are the

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.760
people who are the most insecure, trying

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.320
to signal something or to be somebody

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.840
that they're not, showing that they have

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.600
status or success, and they're quite

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.760
literally spending money on things that

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.560
they don't like to impress people who

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.680
don't give a crap. So, what I do now is

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.760
simple. I wear the same rotation of

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.200
clothes on repeat. And it sounds boring,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.680
I get it, but it's one of the best

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.200
decisions that I've ever made. And

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.800
there's actually a lot of science behind

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.120
this, and a lot of it is related to

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.000
decision fatigue. Every small choice

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.239
that you make throughout the day drains

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.480
your mental energy. What you wear, what

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:49.959
you eat, and what you respond to first

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.600
all adds up over time. The less mental

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:53.640
energy that you burn on things that

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.720
don't matter, the more you have for the

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.720
things that do matter. I literally buy

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:01.360
99% of my clothes from places like Bill

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.160
or cuffs. And no, I'm not sponsored by

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.960
them. I just go ahead, I go on their

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.320
website, I get like five different

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.440
colored shirts like the one that I'm

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.600
wearing, and five different color pants,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.320
and I wear them all year long. And every

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.240
day choosing what I'm going to wear is

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.079
really simple. I just grab something off

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.760
the stack and I'm ready to go for the

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.480
day. The fourth thing is luxury items.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:22.240
So, I'm not going to lie to you. I like

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.200
the finer things in life, just as I'm

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.080
sure you do as well. Look, I want a

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.560
Rolex. I want a McLaren, and I'm not

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.400
going to pretend that I don't. But,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.520
here's how I think about it now. I have

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.000
my whole life to get those things. I

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.120
don't need them for another five or 10

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.800
years. Heck, I don't really ever need

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.880
them. A saying I heard a while back that

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.120
really stuck with me is the hunt is

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.800
better than the kill. Although I know

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.320
I'll buy those things again in my life,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.600
I realized, why am I rushing it? Why do

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.800
I need that stuff right now? My 30s are

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.080
about building a firm foundation.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.520
They're about working hard and

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.720
investing, saving, and setting myself up

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.840
and my family for a better future where

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.160
I don't have to worry about money. I can

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.200
easily delay that gratification into my

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.040
40s or my 50s and let my investments pay

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.960
for those things down the road. Here's

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.480
the bottom line. I won't buy luxury

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.680
items with my earned income. I will only

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.880
buy them moving forward with my passive

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.000
income or income that was generated

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.680
without me trading time for money. All

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.880
right, the fifth thing is eating out and

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.040
having food subscriptions. Think like

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.360
Uber Eats, Postmates, stuff like that.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.640
So, there was a day in my life in my

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.960
early 20s, I was making amazing money,

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.120
and I was eating at expensive

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.280
restaurants all the time. I literally

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.200
had my card on file at Maestro's, and I

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.960
would walk in and people knew me. And

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.560
looking back, that was a really stupid

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.080
choice. I could have invested that

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.400
money, and I'd have a lot more to show

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.520
for it than a fancy steak all the time

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.360
that I could have bought at a meat

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.360
market at a fraction of the cost. And

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.800
what's crazy is the average American

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.080
spends over $1,500 a year just on food

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.240
delivery apps. And that's before you

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.560
realize that you're being quietly taxed

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.600
on every single order. By the time you

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.720
add up the markups, the service fees,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.280
the delivery fees, and the tips, you're

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.360
paying up to 40% more than the menu

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.120
price for the convenience of not having

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.280
to get off of your dang couch. But

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.720
here's a stat that really surprised me.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.560
People are now using Afterpay to pay for

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.800
their meals. Think about that for a

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.760
minute. They're literally financing

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:20.000
food. That is how normalized debt has

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.080
become in our society. What I do now is

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.200
me and my wife simply meal prep our food

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.080
about 90% of the time. We're not

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.240
perfect, but this does multiple things

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.200
for me. Number one, I end up eating

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.080
really similar meals every day, which

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.960
going back to the decision fatigue we

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.560
talked about a little while ago, has a

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.480
lot of benefits cuz I'm not always

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:39.560
thinking about what I have to eat. I end

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.480
up saving a ton of money by doing this,

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.880
and then when I do go out on date night

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.760
or whatever the case is 10% of the time,

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.040
it's actually way more enjoyable because

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.919
it feels like a special occasion, not a

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.280
regular thing I do all the time. Okay,

00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:54.520
number six is buying the newest iPhone

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.760
every year. So, I used to be that guy. I

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:59.280
wanted the newest version of the iPhone

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.000
every September. I would watch videos by

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.880
Marques Brownlee, and the next thing I

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.520
knew I'd be ordering a new iPhone. And

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.280
as I got better with my money and I got

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.560
a little bit older, I realized that was

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.800
really silly. Most of the time there's

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.000
literally no upgrade to my quality of

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.320
life by getting the newest version. From

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.160
generation to generation, these iPhones

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.120
don't really change as much as we think

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.640
that they do. What I do now instead is I

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.200
just skip at least one generation. So,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.919
for example, if I have the iPhone 16,

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.120
I'm not going to upgrade again until the

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:30.320
18 comes out. Sometimes I'll even skip

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.080
two generations just to train myself

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.960
that I don't always need the newest

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.600
latest gadget all the time. Take that

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.760
$1,500 and invest it. Put it in your

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.640
savings. Add it to your emergency fund.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.280
Heck, if you want, put it into a high

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.920
interest CD. Literally doing just about

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.240
anything with that money makes more

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.040
sense than spending it. The seventh

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:52.080
thing that I think is kind of silly, and

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.480
this might make people mad, is expensive

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.240
weddings. So, me and my wife recently

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.680
eloped, and we went to the courthouse.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.760
Completely intentional. No pressure, no

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.720
performance. It was literally me, my

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.400
wife, my grandpa, my grandma, and her

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.520
mom. We looked at what the average

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.720
wedding costs, and it was like 30 to 50

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.480
grand on the low end. And that's for a

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.680
single day. And we made a different

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.280
decision. We bought a house instead. We

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:16.400
bought an asset, something that would

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:18.280
build equity over time, and gave us a

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.440
real foundation to start our life

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.200
together. And we still want to

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.520
celebrate, so when we're ready, we'll

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:27.000
throw a party at our own house, our own

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:29.400
venue. Get this. 67% of newlyweds say

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.600
they took on wedding-related debt, with

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:34.200
many using credit cards or personal

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.520
loans to cover the cost. That means that

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.039
couples are starting their marriage in a

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:41.520
financial hole before their life has

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:44.120
even begun. And it is not a coincidence

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.600
that financial stress constantly ranks

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.640
as one of the leading causes of divorce.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.960
So, you're literally setting yourself up

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.920
to fight about the money that you spent

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:54.839
on the day that you got married. Think

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.839
about that. Now, I'm not telling you to

00:08:54.839 --> 00:08:58.839
not have a wedding. That is a personal

00:08:56.839 --> 00:09:01.320
choice that me and my wife made. But

00:08:58.839 --> 00:09:04.280
going into debt or draining your savings

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:07.080
for one day because of social pressure,

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:09.200
because that's what people do, is really

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.200
one of the most financially destructive

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.080
decisions a young couple can make.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.720
Instead, decide what that day actually

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:18.080
means to you, not to your family, not to

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:21.160
your Instagram, not to social

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.360
expectations, then spend based on that.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.839
Don't spend based on the pressure. All

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.080
right, the final thing that I stopped

00:09:24.839 --> 00:09:29.680
doing is I stopped paying cash for

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:31.560
expensive vacations. So, look, I love to

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.480
travel. Me and my wife have taken some

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.760
incredible trips over the last couple of

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:38.320
years. We recently went to Dubai, we

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.200
went to the Maldives, Thailand, Paris,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.000
London, and I almost never pay cash for

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.840
any of it. If I'm going to pay cash for

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.000
a vacation, then I go somewhere a lot

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.560
more reasonable. We go to Mexico, or we

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.880
take a trip somewhere right here in the

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.400
US. If I want to go on like a luxury

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.440
vacation now, I force myself to be smart

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.360
with how I spend my money. Things I

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.640
would already be spending on, I get

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.040
strategic with, and I put them on credit

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:04.760
cards like the Chase Sapphire card, the

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.360
